### API Endpoint Plan

The RaceDay system will use a RESTful Web API to enable communication between the application and the database. The API will provide endpoints for user authentication, profile management, event management, categories, participant enrolments, results, routes and weather information. HTTP methods such as GET, POST, PUT and DELETE will be used to retrieve, create, update and remove system information. Access to specific endpoints will be controlled according to the user's role. For example, organisers will be responsible for managing events, categories, results, routes and weather information, while participants will be able to register for events and manage their enrolments.

The authentication functionality will allow users to register and log into the RaceDay system using their personal credentials. Once authenticated, users will be able to access and update their profile information. The event endpoints will allow users to view available events, while authorised organisers will be able to create, update and delete events. Categories will be linked to specific events and will contain information such as the race distance, entry fee and age group.

The enrolment endpoints will allow participants to register for specific event categories and view their existing enrolments. Organisers will also be able to view the participants registered for their events. The results endpoints will allow organisers to record and manage participant results, including finish time, chip time, position and pace. Authenticated users will be able to retrieve relevant results. Additional endpoints will provide access to route and weather information associated with events, allowing participants to access useful information before and during an event.

Each API endpoint will define the required HTTP method, route, purpose, user role, request information and expected response. This provides a clear structure for implementing the RaceDay API and ensures that the API corresponds with the entities and relationships identified in the database design. Role-based access also helps protect sensitive operations by ensuring that only authorised users can perform administrative functions.
